Companies often say they want employees to experiment, yet their approval systems are designed for large projects whose outcomes can be predicted in advance. A useful experiment is different: it is deliberately small, answers a specific uncertainty and may produce a result that challenges the original idea. {{1}}

One retail company wanted to know whether customers would pay for same-day delivery. Instead of building a nationwide service, it offered the option in two districts for three weeks, using an existing courier. {{2}}

The test showed strong initial interest but very few completed purchases once the fee appeared. The team resisted the temptation to call the experiment a failure. {{3}}

Small experiments still require discipline. Teams should state what they expect, decide in advance which evidence matters and set a stopping point. {{4}}

Leaders also influence whether uncomfortable results are reported honestly. If praise is reserved for experiments that confirm an idea, employees quickly learn to hide negative evidence. {{5}}

